{"context":"eka-cli ships an executable-based plugin contract (plugin package: `manifest --json`, `install \u003ckind\u003e --json`, Discover over PATH + ~/.eka/plugins). The contract covers discovery and delegation, but not distribution or trust: there is no way for eka-cli to install the official eka-mcp plugin directly, and no way to distinguish official from third-party plugins.","decision":"1. Hardcoded official registry in eka-cli (`mcp -\u003e github.com/maleolabs/eka-mcp`); the registry is the authority of trust, never a plugin's self-declared source. 2. `eka plugin install/list/remove/update` command family for plugin-binary lifecycle. 3. Official install downloads the release asset `eka-mcp-\u003cos\u003e-\u003carch\u003e[.exe]`, verifies SHA-256 against SHA256SUMS.txt (fail-closed, mirroring `eka update`), installs to ~/.eka/plugins, then smoke-checks `manifest --json`. 4. Two-tier trust: official (registry) = full trust + checksum, no prompt; third-party = explicit consent + capability review, still checksum-verified. 5. Manifest extended additively (contract v1) with `source` (informational) and `capabilities` (self-declared, v1 = install + mcp). 6. Trust is enforced at install time only.\n\n### Contract location (amended 2026-08-16, ADR-030)\n\nThe plugin contract types and the official registry live in `eka-core/plugin` (moved from eka-cli/plugin). Consumers import the contract from eka-core only; eka-cli keeps its runner/Discover and imports from eka-core; eka-mcp has no eka-cli dependency.\n","consequences":"- The MCP plugin is distributed through GitHub releases with checksum verification; no circular dependency (eka-cli still knows only the registry + contract). - Third-party plugins are gated by explicit consent and a capability review. - Adding an official plugin requires a CLI release (registry is hardcoded) — acceptable at the current scale.","alternativesConsidered":"- Remote plugin registry (fetched list): rejected — adds a network dependency and a supply-chain surface; the official list is tiny. - No special trust (all plugins identical): rejected — friction for the official plugin and no signal for third-party review. - Run-time trust enforcement (re-check on every manifest/install call): rejected — complexity and latency for marginal gain; install-time review is sufficient."}